## Asymptotic Expansion of \(I(z)=\int_a^b \frac{u(x)}{x-z}\,dx\) as \(|z|\to\infty\)

### Setup and validity of the geometric expansion

Since \(\operatorname{Im} z\neq 0\), the point \(z\) lies off the real axis, so \(x-z\neq 0\) for every \(x\in[a,b]\subset\mathbb{R}\), and the integral is well defined. Write

\[
\frac{1}{x-z}=\frac{1}{-z\,(1-x/z)}=-\frac{1}{z}\cdot\frac{1}{1-x/z}.
\]

For \(|z|>\max(|a|,|b|)\) we have \(|x/z|\le \max(|a|,|b|)/|z|<1\) uniformly for all \(x\in[a,b]\), so the geometric series converges uniformly on \([a,b]\):

\[
\frac{1}{1-x/z}=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\left(\frac{x}{z}\right)^n,\qquad \left|\frac{x}{z}\right|<1.
\]

Hence

\[
\frac{1}{x-z}=-\frac{1}{z}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{z^n}=-\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{z^{n+1}}.
\tag{1}
\]

### Termwise integration

Because the series (1) converges uniformly on \([a,b]\) and \(u\) is integrable (so \(x^n u(x)\) is integrable for every \(n\)), we may integrate term by term:

\[
I(z)=\int_a^b u(x)\left(-\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{z^{n+1}}\right)dx
=-\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{z^{n+1}}\int_a^b x^n\,u(x)\,dx.
\]

Define the **moments** of \(u\):

\[
\boxed{\,m_n:=\int_a^b x^n\,u(x)\,dx,\qquad n=0,1,2,\dots\,}
\]

Then the expansion takes the compact form

\[
\boxed{\,I(z)\sim -\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{m_n}{z^{n+1}}
=-\frac{m_0}{z}-\frac{m_1}{z^2}-\frac{m_2}{z^3}-\cdots,\qquad |z|\to\infty,\ \operatorname{Im}z\neq 0.\,}
\tag{2}
\]

### Asymptotic (not just convergent) character

For fixed \(|z|>\max(|a|,|b|)\) the series (2) is actually convergent. Truncating after the term \(n=N-1\) gives the partial sum

\[
I_N(z)=-\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\frac{m_n}{z^{n+1}},
\]

with remainder

\[
R_N(z)=I(z)-I_N(z)=-\sum_{n=N}^{\infty}\frac{m_n}{z^{n+1}}.
\]

Using \(|m_n|\le\int_a^b |x|^n|u(x)|\,dx\le M\,\max(|a|,|b|)^n\) where \(M=\int_a^b|u(x)|\,dx<\infty\), and setting \(\rho=\max(|a|,|b|)/|z|<1\),

\[
|R_N(z)|\le\frac{M}{|z|}\sum_{n=N}^{\infty}\rho^n=\frac{M}{|z|}\cdot\frac{\rho^N}{1-\rho}
=\frac{M\,\max(|a|,|b|)^N}{|z|^{N+1}\,(1-\max(|a|,|b|)/|z|)}.
\]

Thus for every fixed \(N\),

\[
R_N(z)=O\!\left(|z|^{-(N+1)}\right)\quad\text{as }|z|\to\infty,
\]

which is precisely the defining property of an asymptotic expansion: truncating (2) at any order \(N\) yields an approximation accurate to \(O(|z|^{-(N+1)})\).

### Leading terms and interpretation

Writing out the first few terms explicitly:

\[
I(z)=-\frac{1}{z}\int_a^b u(x)\,dx-\frac{1}{z^2}\int_a^b x\,u(x)\,dx-\frac{1}{z^3}\int_a^b x^2\,u(x)\,dx-\cdots.
\]

The leading term is governed by the total mass \(m_0=\int_a^b u(x)\,dx\); each subsequent term involves the next moment of \(u\). The expansion is valid in the full punctured neighbourhood \(|z|\to\infty\) with \(\operatorname{Im}z\neq 0\) (both upper and lower half-planes), since the only obstruction to the geometric expansion is \(|x/z|<1\), which holds for \(|z|\) large enough regardless of the sign of \(\operatorname{Im}z\).

### Summary

Using \(\displaystyle\frac{1}{x-z}=-\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{z^{n+1}}\) for \(|z|>\max(|a|,|b|)\) and integrating term by term (justified by uniform convergence on \([a,b]\) and integrability of \(u\)), the asymptotic expansion is

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle I(z)=\int_a^b\frac{u(x)}{x-z}\,dx\sim-\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{m_n}{z^{n+1}},\qquad m_n=\int_a^b x^n u(x)\,dx,\qquad |z|\to\infty,\ \operatorname{Im}z\neq 0.}
\]

